.Dd October 6, 2026
.Dt JOB 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm job
.Nd concepts of the job execution and scheduling service
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs commands through a local service,
.Xr jobd 8 ,
so that a command does not depend on the terminal that started it, can wait
for its turn, can be held to stated resources and leaves a record.
This page explains the ideas the commands in
.Xr job 1
and the settings in
.Xr job.conf 5
are built on.
.Sh OBJECTS
There are three kinds of object.
.Bl -tag -width "Queue"
.It Job
One execution of one command with its arguments, environment and working
directory.
A Job has a number that is never used again.
.It Queue
A collection of Jobs.
A Job belongs to exactly one Queue; Jobs submitted without a Queue belong to
the default Queue.
.It Group
A collection of Queues and Groups.
Groups form a tree whose root is
.Pa / .
.El
.Pp
Queues and Groups are named by paths such as
.Pa development/builds .
Renaming or moving one keeps its identity and is refused while Jobs in it run.
A Queue or Group without settings organizes and does nothing else: it adds no
limit, no ordering rule and no kernel resource domain.
.Pp
A Job, a Queue and a Group can carry labels, pairs of a key and a value.
Labels classify without giving an object a second parent: a Job still belongs
to one Queue and a Queue to one Group.
They are never read by scheduling or by any policy and are not inherited.
.Pp
A held or queued Job can be moved to another Queue with
.Cm job move .
It keeps its number, its submission time and its waiting credit, and the
destination is checked as at submission.
.Sh INHERITANCE
A setting on a Queue or Group is either a constraint on its whole subtree or
a default for the Jobs in it.
.Pp
A constraint, such as
.Fl -max-running ,
a priority bound, a shared CPU or memory ceiling, a pause or a closed state,
applies together with the constraints of every ancestor.
A child cannot widen what a parent allows.
.Pp
A default has a name beginning with
.Fl -job- ,
such as
.Fl -job-memory-max .
A Job takes the nearest value on the way from its Queue to the root unless it
states its own.
Defaults are resolved when a Job is submitted, edited or retried; changing a
default later does not rewrite Jobs that already exist.
.Cm unset
removes a local value and lets the inherited one show again.
.Cm show Fl -json
names the source of every effective setting.
.Sh LIFECYCLE AND ATTEMPTS
A Job is created held or submitted directly.
A held Job can be edited and waits for
.Cm release .
A released Job is queued until it is admitted, then it runs, then it ends
with one of four outcomes: success, failure, cancellation or lost execution.
Lost means the service can no longer account for the supervisor, for example
after a reboot; the command may have done part of its work.
.Pp
The states are held, queued, starting, running, suspended, stopping and the
four outcomes succeeded, failed, cancelled and lost.
Records written before the outcomes were told apart carry an eleventh state,
Finished.
No current version writes it.
It is read from old records only and is shown as succeeded when the exit
status was zero and nothing stopped the Job, as cancelled or lost when the
recorded stop says so, and as failed otherwise.
.Pp
.Cm retry
runs a finished Job again under the same number as a new attempt.
Each attempt keeps its own specification, result and retained output.
.Cm cancel
asks for a managed end: a termination signal, and a kill after a deadline.
.Cm signal
delivers one signal to the processes of the workload and decides nothing
else.
.Cm suspend
and
.Cm continue
freeze and thaw a running workload through its cgroup; a suspended Job keeps
its memory and its place.
.Cm remove
deletes the records of finished Jobs and never ends work.
.Pp
Each running Job has a supervisor process of its own.
The supervisor, not the service, owns the command, its output and its
terminal, which is why the service can be restarted while Jobs run.
.Sh SCHEDULING
Admission is the decision that a waiting Job may start.
Without settings, Jobs start in arrival order, and a Job that cannot start yet
does not hold back one that can.
.Pp
The optional rules are separate from each other: a concurrency ceiling
.Pq Fl -max-running ,
a priority from \-1000 to 1000, aging that raises the rank of work that has
waited, strict arrival order within a subtree, backfill protection for an
ordered Job that waits for capacity, and fair share between sibling
collections by requested CPU time or memory time.
.Cm job explain
says why a Job waits.
.Pp
Priority orders waiting work only.
It never preempts a running Job and never changes how the kernel divides CPU
or I/O between running Jobs.
.Sh RESOURCES
Four ideas are kept apart.
.Bl -tag -width "request"
.It request
An amount counted at admission, such as
.Fl -cpu-request
or
.Fl -memory-request .
It reserves nothing in the kernel and guarantees nothing.
.It limit
A ceiling the kernel enforces, such as
.Fl -cpu-limit ,
.Fl -memory-max ,
.Fl -pids-max
for the number of processes and threads, or an I/O rate per device.
.It weight
A relative share under contention, such as
.Fl -cpu-weight .
.It measurement
What a Job actually used, reported with its result.
.El
.Pp
A request sets no limit and a limit makes no request.
A limit or weight on a Group or Queue covers its subtree as one shared
domain; the
.Fl -job-
form gives each Job its own.
Limits and weights need a delegated cgroup with the matching controller.
Where that is missing, a Job that asks for one is refused instead of being
run without it.
.Cm job host Fl -json
and
.Cm job doctor
list what a host offers.
.Pp
The earlier option
.Fl -pids
keeps its meaning: it counts the processes at admission and limits the Job to
them.
.Fl -write-budget ,
earlier
.Fl -disk ,
is a count of bytes written, after which the service stops the Job.
It is not a file system quota; the service sets none and says so in
.Cm job host Fl -json .
.Sh PRESSURE
Linux reports how much time work was stalled for CPU, memory or I/O, as
pressure stall information.
.Cm job pressure
shows it for the host or for one running Job.
.Pp
A pressure rule is optional and explicit.
It names a resource, a threshold and its timing, and while the threshold is
exceeded it holds new starts and later restores concurrency step by step.
A rule never ends or suspends running work, and no rule exists by default.
Pressure says that work was delayed, not what caused the delay.
.Sh PROFILES AND CLASSES
A scheduling class is a named priority.
An execution profile is a named set of defaults, such as requests, limits,
launch settings and a class, that may extend other profiles.
Both carry a revision, are defined in
.Xr job.conf 5
and are selected by name and revision.
A Job records the definition it used, so a later revision does not change
what an earlier attempt meant.
Nothing selects a profile or class implicitly.
.Sh ISOLATION AND SECURITY
Every Job runs as the Unix user of the service.
Jobs of one service are not protected from each other by default.
.Pp
The optional launch settings each do one thing: a private network namespace
.Pq Fl -net Cm none ,
a network reached only through a proxy or tunnel, a bandwidth ceiling,
confinement of file writes with Landlock
.Pq Fl -confine ,
CPU and memory-node placement, per-process resource limits,
.Dv no_new_privs ,
removal of capabilities and a deny list of system calls.
A setting that was asked for and cannot be established refuses the Job
before its command starts.
None of them is a sandbox by itself, and a deny list removes the named calls
and nothing more.
.Sh OUTPUT
The supervisor records standard output and standard error as separate
streams with the time of capture, or one combined stream for a terminal.
.Cm job logs
reads them during and after the run.
Retention is bounded: the beginning and the latest part of a long stream are
kept and the gap between them is reported.
A reader that is slow or gone never stops the command.
.Sh TERMINALS
A Job started with
.Fl -pty
runs on a pseudo-terminal owned by its supervisor.
Any admitted client may
.Cm attach ;
all attached clients see the same screen and may all type.
There is no owner of the input.
Detaching, or losing the connection, leaves the program running.
.Sh ACCESS MODEL
The Unix socket of the service is the access boundary.
A client that is admitted has full control over every Job, Queue, Group and
terminal of that service; there are no roles and no per-object permissions.
Session and creator labels describe, they do not authorize.
.Pp
By default only the service user is admitted.
A service may share its socket with one Unix group; members can then submit
and control work, which still runs as the service user.
The state directory stays private.
A member who cannot read it receives output from the service over the socket
and reaches terminals through sockets in the runtime directory.
The audit journal and the event journal are read from the state directory
and remain with the service user.
See
.Xr jobd 8 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr job 1 ,
.Xr job.conf 5 ,
.Xr jobd 8 ,
.Xr cgroups 7 ,
.Xr landlock 7 ,
.Xr namespaces 7 ,
.Xr pty 7
